chromeos-base/chromeos-initramfs hypervisor_ramfs

# Disable THP in the hypervisor due to its limited utility.
#
# When punching a hole in a THP, freeing memory requires being able to split
# huge pages. However, this is only possible when nothing else in the kernel
# holds a reference to a huge page being split. For the CrOS guest, that
# requirement can be violated if any other base page is pinned by a passthrough
# device. Strict virtio iommu mappings introduce some flakiness, but the fact
# that coiommu caches mappings can make the balloon almost entirely
# ineffective. Although sibling VMs don't have that problem, splitting can
# still be flaky if something else happens to take a concurrently take a ref
# while the balloon is trying to punch a hole (e.g. KVM's call to GUP for
# another subpage after the huge page is unmapped but before it is split).
# These aren't permanent leaks, since the memory will be reused if the subpage
# in question is removed from the balloon. However, until that happens, the
# hypervisor memory service's memory accounting will be wrong.
#
# We could leave THP enabled so other parts of the hypervisor can use it via
# madvise. However, THP also increases watermarks from 4*sqrt(mem) total to
# ~44MiB per zone. This means THP reduces memory for the guests by ~70MiB (or
# ~30MiB if we manage to disable DMA_ZONE32). There are currently no uses cases
# in the hypervisor that justify that memory cost.
sys-kernel/chromeos-kernel-5_4-manatee apply_patches -hypervisor_guest -transparent_hugepage -virtio_balloon
sys-kernel/chromeos-kernel-5_10-manatee apply_patches -hypervisor_guest -transparent_hugepage -virtio_balloon
sys-kernel/chromeos-kernel-5_10-manatee chromeos_kernel_family_manatee disable_kernel_fragments
